{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3276","artifact_type":"architect","name":"plan/architect-contract-verification-async-reporting.md@sha256:bccc8701801293b567965492b7bcd4bcd2e03b1b97dca19dca3ceb2c1d9a0bfd"},"owner_reference":"3276","content":"# Architecture Spec: Contract-Verification Async Reporting (#3276)\n\n**Status**: WHAT to build. No code/prose is written here \u2014 exact section names, tool calls,\nschema fields, and edit boundaries only. Implementation (the literal markdown prose) is a\nlater task's job.\n\n**Ground rule driving every decision below**: two independent defect sites exist\n(`contract-verification.md` / `implement-feature/SKILL.md` step 4a, and `groom/error.md`'s\nAgent Failure procedure). They are fixed with the *same pattern* (agent writes its own result\nto a backend destination named in its own agent file; the consumer reads it back) but not the\n*same destination* \u2014 each site's destination is chosen to match its own consumption shape,\nper `subagent-contract/SKILL.md`'s primary rule (\"put your result where your own agent file\nsays\"). Forcing one mechanism onto both sites would be the over-generalization Impact Radius\nalready warns against (R5).\n\n---\n\n## 1. `agents/contract-verification.md`\n\n### 1.1 Mechanism selection: `backlog_groom` on the Concerns section \u2014 not `artifact_register`\n\n**Decision**: contract-verification writes directly via\n`mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom(selector=\"#{issue}\", section=\"Concerns\", content=<entry>, append=True)`.\nThis is the same call `implement-feature/SKILL.md` step 4a already issues today (lines 139-145\nand 190-196) \u2014 the fix moves *who* issues it (the agent, not the orchestrator), not *where it\ngoes*.\n\n**This diverges from Design Intent Alignment's lean toward `artifact_register`.** That section\nargues `artifact_register` is \"the correct routing by the fallback contract\" (subagent-contract's\n\"every other document is an artifact\" clause) and cites `t0-baseline-capture` /\n`tn-verification-gate` as proof. Both claims are true in isolation but the conclusion doesn't\nfit this site, for three concrete reasons:\n\n1. **No artifact_type fits.** `artifact_register`'s `artifact_type` parameter is a closed enum:\n   `feature-context, architect, task-plan, T0-baseline, TN-verification, codebase-analysis,\n   code-review, research, dispatch-plan, audit-report`. None names \"per-task contract\n   findings.\" `code-review` is the nearest semantic neighbor but is already owned by\n   `dh:code-reviewer`'s S6 forensic-review schema (PASS/FAIL/NEEDS-WORK) \u2014 reusing it would\n   make `artifact_read(item_id, \"code-review\")` (no `artifact_id`) return whichever of the two\n   producers wrote most recently, silently corrupting the other consumer. Extending the enum is\n   a backend/Python change; out of scope (five markdown files only, per the dispatch context for\n   this spec).\n2. **The consumption shape doesn't match T0/TN's.** T0/TN write a single structured YAML blob\n   consumed *once* by exactly one downstream step (TN reads T0; `/complete-implementation` reads\n   TN). Contract-verification's findings are *accumulating, human-facing, actionable checklist\n   items* \u2014 the same shape as every other concern already flowing into the Concerns section from\n   step 4's own `<concerns>` block handling (lines 136-145). An artifact would be a second,\n   differently-shaped channel for content that already has a working, precedented, checklist-shaped\n   home.\n3. **AC1 and AC2 name the destination already.** Acceptance Criteria 1-2 both say \"the backlog\n   item's Concerns section (or equivalent durable, inspectable location)\" \u2014 Concerns is the\n   criteria's own first-named destination, not a fallback.\n\n`subagent-contract/SKILL.md`'s primary rule \u2014 \"put your result where your own agent file says\" \u2014\nis satisfied by naming Concerns explicitly in `contract-verification.md`'s own Output Format\nsection. That rule takes priority over the artifact-register fallback, which activates only\n\"where neither [the agent file nor the dispatch] names one\" (see \u00a75 below: this is why\n`subagent-contract` needs no edit).\n\n### 1.2 New required input: `issue_number`\n\nAdd to the **Inputs** section (currently lines 23-32, which lists `architect_spec_path`,\n`task_id`, `modified_files`):\n\n- `issue_number` \u2014 the parent backlog item's identifier (`str | int` \u2014 GitHub integer ID or\n  beads nanoid string), needed to address `backlog_groom(selector=\"#{issue_number}\", ...)`.\n\nAdd to the BLOCKED condition already present (\"If any input is missing... return BLOCKED\nimmediately\"): `issue_number` missing is a BLOCKED condition, mirroring\n`t0-baseline-capture.md`'s treatment of its own required `item_id` input\n(`agents/t0-baseline-capture.md:22`, `:146`).\n\n### 1.3 Replace the Output Format section (lines 121-151)\n\nKeep the concerns-block *construction* logic (Steps 1-5, lines 34-119) unchanged \u2014 extraction\nand comparison are not part of this defect. Replace only how the result is delivered:\n\n**Remove** (line 151): `Your finding is your response. The dispatcher reads it there \u2014 you\nregister no artifact and write no task state.`\n\n**Replace with** a procedure that, after constructing the concerns list (possibly empty),\nalways issues exactly one `backlog_groom` call per finding, plus exactly one call when the\nlist is empty \u2014 never zero calls:\n\n- **When mismatches are found**: for each concern entry, call\n  `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom(selector=\"#{issue_number}\", section=\"Concerns\", content=\"- [ ] CONTRACT: {severity} \u2014 {issue line, one sentence} (reported by contract-verification on {task_id})\", append=True)`.\n  This is the exact content shape `implement-feature/SKILL.md` step 4a currently builds at\n  lines 190-196 \u2014 moved into the producer.\n- **When no mismatches are found**: call the same operation once with a pre-resolved (checked)\n  entry so silence is never the encoding for \"clean\": `content=\"- [x] CONTRACT: no concerns \u2014\n  all contracts in scope satisfied (reported by contract-verification on {task_id})\"`. The\n  leading `[x]` (vs `[ ]` for an open violation) is the interface contract that distinguishes a\n  clean run from a dropped one at a glance, without adding a second section or a second tool\n  call convention \u2014 this satisfies AC2 with the same call shape already used for AC1.\n- **Terminal response**: end with `STATUS: DONE` (per `dh:subagent-contract`, already a skill\n  this agent loads) summarizing what was written \u2014 e.g. `STATUS: DONE\\n\\nWrote N contract\n  finding(s) to #{issue_number} Concerns section (task {task_id}).` The response text itself is\n  no longer load-bearing for delivering the finding \u2014 the write already landed before the agent\n  returns. Downstream, nothing needs to parse this text (see \u00a72.3).\n- **BLOCKED case unchanged in kind**: still applies when `architect_spec_path` doesn't resolve,\n  now also when `issue_number` is absent (\u00a71.2).\n\n### 1.4 Tools frontmatter (line 5)\n\nDrop `SendMessage` from the `tools:` grant. It is unused after this change (no instruction\ncalls it, matching feature-context Q6's observation that the grant survived `11082f0eb`'s\nremoval of the instruction that used it), and its presence is exactly the kind of unused,\ndiscoverable tool surface that invites the next author to reach for the wrong precedent\n(`SendMessage(to=\"team-lead\")`) the way this defect's own history shows happened for\n`impact-analyst`. New frontmatter: `tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash, Skill, mcp__plugin_dh_sam,\nmcp__plugin_dh_backlog`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. `skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` step 4a\n\n### 2.1 Dispatch prompt: add `issue_number`\n\nThe existing `Agent(subagent_type=\"dh:contract-verification\", ...)` call (lines 166-185)\nalready runs inside the `if a parent issue number is known` branch (line 151) \u2014 the value\nexists at dispatch time under whatever local name step 4a already uses for it (`{issue}`,\nconsistent with section 4's own `backlog_groom(selector=\"#{issue}\", ...)` at line 141). Add one\nline to the delegation prompt: `Issue number: {issue}` (or equivalent), matching the new\nrequired input from \u00a71.2.\n\n### 2.2 Remove the synchronous branch (lines 187-198)\n\nDelete this entire block:\n\n> If the contract-verification agent returns a `<concerns>` block, append each concern to the\n> backlog item with a `CONTRACT:` prefix: [backlog_groom call] ... Use the MCP tool for this\n> call.\n\nThis responsibility now lives inside `contract-verification.md` (\u00a71.3). Step 4a's job shrinks\nto: dispatch, and stop depending on anything about *what* comes back.\n\n### 2.3 What step 4a still needs from the dispatch: a completion signal, not a completion value\n\nThis resolves Open Question Q2 from the feature-context, and is the reason no new\npolling/waiting infrastructure is introduced. Two facts, once combined, make the answer trivial:\n\n1. The payload no longer travels through the `Agent()` return channel at all (\u00a71.3) \u2014 it lands\n   in the Concerns section via `backlog_groom` before the agent's `STATUS: DONE` response is\n   even produced.\n2. Nothing in step 4a *itself* consumes the concerns immediately. The existing line 149 already\n   states: \"Concerns accumulate across all task agents. They feed into the validation stage in\n   `/complete-implementation`\" \u2014 a materially later step, not step 4a or 4b.\n\nTherefore step 4a requires no new read-back call and no new wait/poll loop: dispatch\n`dh:contract-verification` the same way it is dispatched today (bare `Agent()`, no `TeamCreate`,\nper the item's explicit exclusion), and proceed to step 4b once the dispatch call returns \u2014\nwhatever async acknowledgment or idle notification the runtime supplies for that dispatch is\nnow merely a \"the agent ran\" signal, not a data channel, so its previous unreliability as a data\nchannel (the entire bug) stops mattering. The one requirement carried forward: `/complete-implementation`'s\npre-existing Concerns-reading step (unchanged by this spec) executes after contract-verification\nhas had the opportunity to finish writing \u2014 already true today, since bookend TN verification and\nthe Completion Gate (`implement-feature/SKILL.md` lines 346-349) already sit between the last\ntask's step 4a and `/complete-implementation`'s dispatch, providing the same ordering guarantee\nthe T0/TN artifacts rely on. No new sequencing mechanism is introduced; the existing one is\nreused for a second consumer.\n\n---\n\n## 3. `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` \u2014 Agent Failure procedure\n\nSame pattern, different destination, because this site's consumption shape is single-shot\nstructured diagnostic data feeding one decision (T0/TN's shape), not an accumulating checklist\n(Concerns' shape).\n\n### 3.1 Mechanism selection: `artifact_register` with `artifact_type=\"audit-report\"`\n\n`audit-report` is the existing enum member closest in kind to \"diagnostic review of a failed\nagent's session\" and is not already owned by a conflicting producer within the same item\n(unlike `code-review`). This is the correct fit for this site precisely because\n`t0-baseline-capture.md` / `tn-verification-gate.md` are the correct precedent *here* \u2014 single\nstructured record, consumed once, immediately, by the same workflow that produced it.\n\n**Fixed identifier, not a generated one**: `artifact_id=\"audit-report-groom-agent-failure\"`\n(stable string, not `{timestamp}`-suffixed). `artifact_register` is documented as idempotent by\n`(artifact_type, artifact_id)` \u2014 a second diagnostic dispatch on a retry overwrites the first in\nplace. That is the desired behavior: the Agent Failure procedure only ever needs the *latest*\ndiagnostic to drive its immediate next branch, never a history of past diagnostics for the same\nitem. (Ceiling: if concurrent grooming diagnostics for the same item ever become possible, this\nsingle slot would need per-dispatch uniqueness \u2014 not indicated anywhere in the current grooming\nworkflow, which is single-threaded per item.)\n\n### 3.2 Diagnostic content schema\n\nThe dispatched `dh:task-worker` agent (step 3, lines 17-26) currently is asked to \"Report:\nlast_success, first_failure, failure_type\" in its response text. Replace \"Report:\" with an\ninstruction to assemble and register this YAML content (in memory, no file write \u2014 same\ndiscipline as T0/TN):\n\n```yaml\ndiagnosed_at: \"<ISO 8601 UTC>\"\nstage: \"<the step/stage the failed agent was executing>\"\nlast_success: \"<last successful tool call or output, or null>\"\nfirst_failure: \"<first error, timeout, or missing output, or null>\"\nfailure_type: \"interrupted | tool_error | unknown\"\n```\n\nRegistered via:\n\n```text\nmcp__plugin_dh_backlog__artifact_register(\n    item_id=<item_ref>,\n    artifact_type=\"audit-report\",\n    artifact_id=\"audit-report-groom-agent-failure\",\n    content=<yaml_string>,\n    agent=\"task-worker\"\n)\n```\n\n`<item_ref/>` is already available in this file's context (used at line 51 for\n`backlog update --selector \"<item_ref/>\"`), so no new input needs to be threaded in \u2014 only the\nexisting value needs to be included in the diagnostic agent's delegation prompt (it currently\nis not).\n\n### 3.3 Insert a read-back before step 4's decision tree\n\nBetween the dispatch (step 3, ends line 26) and \"4. Based on the diagnostic:\" (line 28), insert\nan explicit read-back:\n\n```text\nmcp__plugin_dh_backlog__artifact_read(item_id=<item_ref>, artifact_type=\"audit-report\", artifact_id=\"audit-report-groom-agent-failure\")\n```\n\nParse the returned YAML for `last_success`, `first_failure`, `failure_type`. The Mermaid\nflowchart at lines 30-40 is otherwise unchanged \u2014 it already branches on a variable named\n`failure_type`; that variable's source changes from \"the diagnostic agent's return value\" to\n\"the parsed field from the read-back,\" nothing else. This is what makes Acceptance Criterion 4\n(\"a scenario engineered to hit the 2nd-failure condition actually reaches Escalation,\nobservably\") true: the branch condition now evaluates against a real, durably-read value instead\nof a value that never arrived.\n\nIf `artifact_read` returns no content (diagnostic agent failed to register, or was itself\ninterrupted before registering): route to this same file's own **System Error** procedure\n(\u00a73, \"An MCP tool returned an error dict... a required tool is unavailable\") rather than\nguessing a `failure_type` \u2014 this reuses an existing, already-specified escape hatch in the same\nfile instead of inventing a new one.\n\n---\n\n## 4. `skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md`\n\n### 4.1 What the current sentence gets right (must survive)\n\n`<dispatch_selection>`'s subject, per the file's own frontmatter description, is \"which agent a\ndh workflow dispatches\" \u2014 specialist selection, not result routing. The clause \"any specialist\nthat fits runs it, dh roster or not\" (for a dispatch handing over no dh operation) is a true and\nstill-needed statement: a review-only dispatch genuinely does not require a `dh:` specialist or\n`dh:task-worker` fallback, because there is no SAM task/artifact operation that only a dh-aware\nagent could perform. This selection guidance is orthogonal to the defect and must not be\nweakened or removed.\n\n### 4.2 What must be removed: the implicit reporting claim\n\nThe defect is entirely carried by one embedded phrase: **\"whose finding is its response... has\nnothing here to reach.\"** This is not a specialist-selection statement \u2014 it is an (incorrect)\nclaim about where results go, smuggled into a sentence whose stated topic is agent selection.\nIt is the exact doctrine `contract-verification.md` cited (in effect, if not by literal\ncross-reference) to justify its line-151 override, and per Root-Cause Analysis this pattern\nrecurs across 6 agents \u2014 leaving this phrase standing regenerates the defect for the next\nagent author regardless of how many individual agents get fixed (Impact Radius R2).\n\n**Required change in kind** (exact prose is an implementation decision, not specified here):\nsplit the one sentence into its two independent claims, keep the true one, delete the false one,\nand replace the deleted claim with an explicit cross-reference rather than a silent gap \u2014 e.g.\nthe specialist-selection clause stands alone, and a new adjacent clause states that regardless of\nwhich specialist is chosen, *where the finding goes* is never decided by this file \u2014 it is\ngoverned exclusively by `subagent-contract/SKILL.md`'s `<result_destination>` clause, every\ntime, including for review-only dispatches with \"nothing [t]here to reach\" in the specialist-selection\nsense. The phrase \"has nothing here to reach\" may survive only if re-scoped unambiguously to mean\n\"no dh roster requirement,\" never \"no destination requirement.\"\n\n### 4.3 `<artifact_consumption>` clause \u2014 unaffected\n\n\"Every artifact is consumed as an input by a later step. An artifact no step reads is a missing\nconsumer, not a redundant artifact\" (lines 22-27) is orthogonal to this defect and needs no\nchange \u2014 it is already the invariant \u00a73.3's read-back satisfies (the `audit-report` artifact\ngains a real consumer: error.md step 4) and \u00a71 deliberately does not violate (Concerns-section\nentries are not artifacts, so this clause does not apply to them, and should not be stretched to\ncover them).\n\n---\n\n## 5. `skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md` \u2014 confirmed: no change\n\nGrooming's hypothesis is correct, for a more specific reason than \"the fallback is already\ncorrect\": the fallback clause doesn't even activate for either fixed site, because both fixed\nagent files now name their destination explicitly, and `<result_destination>`'s *primary* rule\n\u2014 \"Put your result where your own agent file says\" \u2014 resolves the routing before the fallback\n(\"every other document is an artifact...\") is ever reached. The fallback text was written to\ncover callers whose agent file is silent on destination; after this fix, `contract-verification.md`\nand the `groom/error.md` diagnostic dispatch are no longer silent, so the fallback's scope\nshrinks back to callers this spec doesn't touch. No edit needed.\n\n**One observation, not a required change**: the fallback's enumerated destination list\n(\"repository files... `artifact_register`... SAM plan and task operations\") does not explicitly\nname \"a named section of the calling item, via `backlog_groom`\" as a fourth category, even\nthough \u00a71 of this spec relies on exactly that category being valid under the primary rule. This\nis not a defect \u2014 the primary rule doesn't require the fallback list to enumerate every possible\nagent-file-specified destination \u2014 but a future editor tightening this file should be aware the\ncategory exists and is now in active use by `contract-verification.md`.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Scope boundary confirmation (Acceptance Criterion 5, #3250)\n\nNo file touched by this spec dispatches via `TeamCreate`, references `team-lead`, or adds/removes\na `SendMessage(to=\"team-lead\", ...)` call. `contract-verification.md` loses a `SendMessage`\ngrant it never used (\u00a71.4); nothing in this spec adds one. #3250's audit scope (TeamCreate\nteammates missing a `SendMessage`-to-team-lead completion step) is untouched by every change\nabove, and every change above is verifiable by re-running the reproduction steps in the item's\nReproducibility section without reference to #3250's fix \u2014 the two remain independently\nverifiable as the item requires.\n\n## 7. Explicitly deferred (not in this spec's scope)\n\n- **Q7 / Root-Cause Analysis's \"Improvement\" recommendation** (a documentation-and-review gate\n  requiring all `dh:*` agents to declare a completion-report step, plus a follow-up audit of the\n  remaining ~4 agents named in the frequency analysis): not in this item's five Acceptance\n  Criteria. Recommend a separate follow-up backlog item; do not fold it into this fix (Effort\n  section sizes this item Small-to-Medium against a known, enumerated file set \u2014 a general\n  guard is a different, larger item).\n- **`add-new-feature/SKILL.md`**: independently verified clean by Impact Radius (system 8) and\n  the feature-context (\u00a7Out of scope) \u2014 zero bare `Agent(` calls, already artifact-register-based.\n  Not touched.\n"}